# Issue Content Router

Use this file before creating a beads issue. Its job is to choose the issue
type, collect the minimum intake fields, and then load the matching
type-specific rules.

## Common Contract

Initial descriptions should be intentionally lean. Record the minimum
self-contained contract needed to start work; do not pre-fill implementation
detail, speculative alternatives, or discoveries that have not happened yet.

The description must let an executor who has not seen the conversation proceed
without asking follow-up questions. Record only constraints that are already
agreed and would change execution.

Before `bd create`, re-read the description and confirm a reader without
conversation context could proceed. If any required intake field is missing, or
if unresolved choices would change execution, ask the user before creating the
issue.

## Type Selection

Choose the issue type before writing the description. After choosing the type,
read the linked detail rules and use that file's headings.

| Type | Use when | Required intake before create | Detail rules |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `bug` | Existing behavior is broken. | Observed behavior, expected behavior, evidence or reproduction signal, affected scope. | [issue-content-bug.md](issue-content-bug.md) |
| `task` | A concrete work item changes implementation, tests, docs, or structure and is not better described as a bug, feature, chore, or grouping issue. | Target change, agreed approach or constraints, acceptance or verification criteria. | [issue-content-task.md](issue-content-task.md) |
| `feature` | New functionality or capability is being added, or a feature-level parent is needed to group related executable work. | First decide executable feature vs grouping feature; then collect the matching fields from the detail rules. | [issue-content-feature.md](issue-content-feature.md) |
| `chore` | Maintenance, operational upkeep, documentation/reference artifacts, release preparation, cleanup, or tooling upkeep. | Artifact or operation, purpose, consumer or audience, acceptance criteria. | [issue-content-chore.md](issue-content-chore.md) |
| `epic` | A larger initiative groups multiple features. | Objective, feature grouping principle, completion criteria. | [issue-content-epic.md](issue-content-epic.md) |

## Executable vs Grouping

An issue is executable when it has a concrete deliverable and acceptance
criteria that can be verified directly. `bug`, `task`, `feature`, and `chore`
can all be executable.

An issue is grouping-oriented when it can only be completed by creating or
closing child issues. `epic` is grouping-oriented by default. `feature` may be
grouping-oriented when it represents a parent for several executable issues.

Do not mix executable instructions and child-issue grouping in one `feature`.
If both are needed, create a grouping `feature` and separate executable child
issues.

## Issue Hierarchy

Do not create hierarchy by default. For directly executable work, create a
single `bug`, `task`, `feature`, or `chore`. Use hierarchy only when the work
needs grouping:

- `feature` groups multiple executable issues for one independently reviewable
  capability.
- `epic` groups multiple features for one larger initiative.
- When hierarchy is used, prefer `epic -> grouping feature -> executable child
  issue`, where executable child issue usually means `bug`, `task`, or `chore`.
  Use an executable `feature` as its own issue when the deliverable is an
  independently reviewable capability rather than a grouping parent.

Avoid attaching executable issues directly under an epic unless no meaningful
feature grouping exists.

## Priority

`0` critical / `1` high / `2` medium (default) / `3` low / `4` backlog.
